crossing rate formalizes cross-layer prediction disagreement~\citep{kaya2019shallow,zhou2020bert}, and additive composite. Given $N$ test traces and a probe at layer~$\ell$ producing step-level scores $s_t^{(\ell)}$, a \emph{first crossing point} (FCP) exists for trace~$i$ when $s_{t'}^{(\ell)} > \tau$ for $K$ consecutive steps. The crossing rate is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{CR}(\ell) \;=\; \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \mathbb{1}\!\bigl[\mathrm{FCP}_i^{(\ell)} \text{ exists}\bigr].
\label{eq:crossing_rate}
\end{equation}
A multiplicative composite (BA $\times$ crossing rate) serves as the \emph{crossing-rate composite} (CR-composite) baseline, so named because it incorporates crossing rate in the selection criterion.

